/vendor/
/var/cache/
/var/*.log
.env
*.bak
*.tmp
var/locks/

# Visitor PII. This must never reach a git remote: the content repository is
# pushed hourly, and a name, an email and a message replicated to a remote with
# no retention limit and no erasure path is the GDPR problem this whole
# directory layout exists to avoid. Backed up separately, encrypted, 30 days.
var/submissions/

# content/ is deliberately NOT ignored — not uploads.
#
# They used to be. public/uploads/ was gitignored and nothing copied it
# anywhere, so a lost box restored every page perfectly with every image on it
# broken. Uploads are hash-suffixed, so replacing an image writes a new blob
# instead of rewriting an old one, which is the usual reason binaries in git go
# bad. Thirty to eighty images at store_max_edge is 25–100 MB: nothing.
#
# This stops being right at a gallery site or past a few hundred MB. That site
# sets R2_ENABLED=1 and the paths stop being local; see README "The ceiling".

# Revisions ARE tracked in a site's content repository, where they are the
# restore points a client actually needs. This is not that repository — it is
# the demo's *code* repo, and these are one developer's undo history, rewritten
# on every save. Tracking them here is churn on every local edit and restores
# nothing that content/pages/ does not already hold.
/content/.revisions/

# Superseded by theme/demo.pen, which is larger, newer, and the copy the theme
# repo publishes. Kept on disk rather than deleted until it is confirmed dead.
/demo.pen

# Generated asset bundles: content-addressed, rebuilt on demand.
/public/assets/
